import json,re
E=[]
def a(key,head,sk,text): E.append({"key":key,"headword":head,"sort_key":sk,"entry":text.strip()})

a("Earthquake_5143a0","Earthquake","earthquake","""Shaking of the Earth's surface resulting from a sudden release of energy in the [[Lithosphere|lithosphere]] that creates seismic waves. Also called a quake, tremor, or temblor, an earthquake ranges from events too weak to be felt to those violent enough to throw objects and people into the air and destroy entire cities. The initial point of rupture is the hypocenter, or focus; the point at ground level directly above it is the epicenter.

Most earthquakes are caused by the rupture of geological [[Fault (geology)|faults]], including normal, reverse (thrust), and strike-slip faults. Volcanic activity, landslides, mine blasts, and nuclear tests can also produce them, and human activities such as mining, fracking, and fluid injection can induce earthquakes, which typically release far less energy than major natural events. Under the elastic-rebound theory, the sides of a fault lock at irregularities, strain builds as the plates keep moving, and the stored energy is released suddenly when the stress breaks through. Over 90% of earthquakes occur in the circum-Pacific seismic belt or along other convergent and transform margins.

Size is measured on the moment magnitude scale, which supersedes the [[Richter scale]] for larger events. Shaking intensity, which varies with distance from the epicenter, soil conditions, and building quality, is assessed with scales such as the [[Modified Mercalli intensity scale|Modified Mercalli intensity scale]]. Effects include ground shaking, soil liquefaction, and landslides; when a large earthquake occurs offshore, the seabed may be displaced enough to cause a [[Tsunami|tsunami]].

The [[1960 Valdivia earthquake|1960 earthquake]] in Chile, at 9.5 magnitude, is the largest measured on a seismograph. The [[1976 Tangshan earthquake|1976 Tangshan earthquake]] in China, which killed between 240,000 and 655,000 people, was the deadliest of the 20th century. The [[1556 Shaanxi earthquake|1556 Shaanxi earthquake]] killed more than 100,000 people, many of them in collapsing dwellings carved out of loess hillsides. Precise prediction remains elusive; risk management relies on forecasting, preparedness, seismic retrofitting, and earthquake engineering.""")

a("East_Asia_f74e1c","East Asia","east asia","""Geocultural region of Asia comprising [[China]], [[Japan]], [[Mongolia]], [[North Korea]], [[South Korea]], and [[Taiwan]], plus two special administrative regions of China, [[Hong Kong]] and Macau. It borders North Asia to the north, Southeast Asia to the south, South Asia to the southwest, and Central Asia to the west; the Pacific Ocean lies to its east. The region covers approximately 7.5 million sq km.

East Asia is inhabited by around 1.65 billion people, nearly 33% of the population of continental Asia and 20% of the global population. The overall density is 133 inhabitants per sq km, about three times the world average of 45. Coastal and riverside areas are among the most densely populated places on Earth, while Mongolia and western China are sparsely settled; Mongolia has the lowest population density of any sovereign state. The economies of China, Japan, South Korea, and Taiwan are among the world's largest, fastest-growing, and most prosperous.

Chinese civilization is regarded as one of the earliest cradles of civilization. For thousands of years China was the leading civilization in the region, and East Asian societies have fallen within its sphere of influence. Under Emperor Wu, the [[Han dynasty]] became the regional power; its expansion in 108 BC brought the northern part of the Korean peninsula under Chinese control as the province of Lelang. East Asian vocabularies and scripts are often derived from Classical Chinese and the Chinese script, and many East Asian calendars derive from the Chinese calendar. Other civilizations, such as Tibet, Manchuria, and Ryukyu (Okinawa), were later absorbed into neighboring ones.

Major religions include Buddhism (mostly Mahayana), Confucianism, Taoism, and Chinese folk religion in China, Hong Kong, Macau, and Taiwan; Shinto in Japan; and Christianity and Musok in Korea. Mandarin Chinese, Japanese, and Korean are the major languages. The region has 76 officially recognized minority or indigenous ethnic groups, 55 of them native to mainland China.""")

a("Eastern_Orthodox_Church_610bd0","Eastern Orthodox Church","eastern orthodox church","""Second-largest Christian body in the world after the [[Catholic Church|Roman Catholic Church]], officially the Orthodox Catholic Church and also known as the Greek Orthodox Church. It is one of the six major doctrinal and jurisdictional groups of Christianity, with an estimated 260 to 300 million adherents.

The church is a communion of autocephalous churches, each governed by its bishops through local synods. It has no central doctrinal or governmental authority analogous to the [[Pope|pope]]; the Ecumenical Patriarch of [[Constantinople]] is recognized as *primus inter pares* ("first among equals"), a title held by the patriarch of Rome before the [[East–West Schism]]. Its theology rests on the Scriptures and holy tradition, which includes the decrees of the seven ecumenical councils and the teaching of the Church Fathers. The church holds that it is the one, holy, catholic, and apostolic church established by Jesus Christ and that its bishops are successors of the apostles. It recognizes seven major sacraments, called holy mysteries, of which the [[Eucharist]] is the principal one; through a priest's consecration, the bread and wine become the body and blood of Christ. The Virgin Mary is venerated as the Theotokos ("God-bearer"), and the veneration of icons is central to worship. Its biblical canon contains 76 to 79 books, depending on local tradition.

The churches of Constantinople, Alexandria, Jerusalem, and Antioch shared communion with the Church of Rome until the East–West Schism in 1054, the culmination of theological, political, and cultural disputes, particularly over the authority of the pope. Since 2018 an ongoing schism has divided Constantinople and [[Moscow]], which are not in full communion.

Eastern Orthodoxy is the primary religious confession in [[Russia]], Ukraine, Romania, Greece, Belarus, Serbia, Bulgaria, Georgia, Moldova, North Macedonia, Cyprus, and Montenegro. Roughly half of Eastern Orthodox Christians live in post-Eastern Bloc countries, mostly in Russia. Communities in North America, Western Europe, and Australia arose through diaspora, conversion, and missionary activity.""")

a("Eastern_philosophy_b37396","Eastern philosophy","eastern philosophy","""Collective term, also Asian or Oriental philosophy, for the philosophies that originated in East, Southeast, and South Asia. It includes [[Chinese philosophy]], Japanese, Korean, and Vietnamese philosophy, which are dominant in East Asia, and [[Indian philosophy]], which is dominant in South Asia, Southeast Asia, Tibet, Japan, and Mongolia.

Indian philosophy comprises the ancient traditions (*darshana*, "world views") of the Indian subcontinent, including [[Hindu philosophy]], Jain philosophy, and [[Buddhist philosophy]]. Some of the earliest surviving philosophical texts are the [[Upanishads]] of the later [[Vedas|Vedic]] period (1000–500 BC). Central concepts include dharma, karma, samsara, moksha, and ahimsa. Indian thinkers developed systems of epistemology (*pramana*) and logic, investigated ontology, and wrote on political philosophy, as in the *Arthashastra* (c. 4th century BC), and on love, as in the *Kama Sutra*.

Schools are classified as orthodox (*astika*) or heterodox (*nastika*), depending on whether they accept the Vedas as a valid source of knowledge, the premises of Brahman and Atman, or an afterlife. The six major orthodox schools are Nyaya, Vaisheshika, Samkhya, Yoga, Mimamsa, and Vedanta; the five major heterodox schools are Jain, Buddhist, Ajivika, Ajnana, and Carvaka. Samkhya is a dualist tradition holding that the universe consists of two realities, *purusha* (consciousness) and *prakriti* (matter). The closely related Yoga school emphasized meditation and liberation and, unlike most Samkhya thinkers, allowed for the existence of God. Later developments included Tantra, Iranian-Islamic influences, and the early modern flourishing of Navya-Nyaya ("new reason"). Buddhism mostly disappeared from India after the Muslim conquest, surviving in the Himalayan regions and south India.

Chinese philosophy arose chiefly during the Zhou dynasty. Along with Indian thought, it developed during the Axial Age of the 6th to 5th centuries BC, when figures such as the Buddha and [[Confucius]] articulated paths to self-realization and social harmony through meditation, moral cultivation, and alignment with cosmic principles such as the Tao.""")

a("Ecology_879605","Ecology","ecology","""Natural science that studies the interactions between organisms and their environments. It considers organisms at the community, ecosystem, and biosphere levels and overlaps with biogeography, evolutionary biology, genetics, and ethology.

Ecology is a branch of [[Biology|biology]] concerned with the abundance, biomass, and distribution of organisms in their environmental context. It encompasses life processes, interactions, and adaptations; the movement of materials and energy through living communities; the successional development of ecosystems; cooperation, competition, and predation within and between species; and patterns of biodiversity and their effect on ecosystem processes. The term, from Ancient Greek *oîkos* ("house") and *-logía* ("study of"), was coined in 1866 by the German scientist [[Ernst Haeckel]]. The science as it is known today began with a group of American botanists in the 1890s, and concepts of adaptation and [[Natural selection|natural selection]] are cornerstones of modern ecological theory.

To make the field manageable, the biological world is organized into a hierarchy running from organisms to populations, guilds, communities, [[Ecosystem|ecosystems]], [[Biome|biomes]], and the [[Biosphere|biosphere]]. The hierarchy exhibits non-linear behavior: small changes to critical variables, such as the number of nitrogen fixers, can lead to disproportionate and perhaps irreversible changes in the system. The two main subdisciplines differ in paradigm. Population (or community) ecology focuses on the distribution and abundance of organisms, while ecosystem ecology focuses on fluxes of materials and energy. Ecosystems, in turn, vary from tiny to vast: several generations of an aphid population can live and die on a single leaf, and each aphid supports diverse bacterial communities.

Several concepts are central to the field. [[Biodiversity]] describes the diversity of life from genes to ecosystems and includes species, ecosystem, and genetic diversity. A species' [[Habitat|habitat]] is the environment over which it occurs and the type of community that forms there. The niche concept dates to 1917; in 1957 G. Evelyn Hutchinson defined it as the set of biotic and abiotic conditions in which a species can persist and maintain stable population sizes, and it is divided into fundamental and realized niches. Organisms also modify their habitats, from a beaver pond to decaying logs, a process called niche construction. Biomes categorize regions of the Earth's ecosystems mainly by the structure and composition of their vegetation and include tropical rainforest, temperate forests, taiga, tundra, hot desert, and polar desert.

Ecology has practical applications in conservation biology, wetland management, natural resource management, and human ecology. Studies of fishery collapses caused by overexploitation exceeding reproductive rates have informed resource management.""")

a("Economics_7e1e3f","Economics","economics","""[[Social science]] that studies the production, distribution, and consumption of goods and services. It examines the behavior and interactions of economic agents and how economies work. [[Microeconomics]] analyzes individual agents, such as households, firms, buyers, and sellers, and markets; [[Macroeconomics|macroeconomics]] analyzes economies as systems in which production, distribution, consumption, savings, and investment interact, along with the factors of production (labor, capital, land, and enterprise), inflation, economic growth, and public policy. Other broad distinctions are between positive economics, which describes "what is," and normative economics, which advocates "what ought to be"; between economic theory and applied economics; between rational and behavioral economics; and between mainstream and heterodox economics. Economic analysis is applied to business, finance, health care, government, and such subjects as crime, education, the family, law, politics, religion, war, and the environment.

The earlier term was "political economy"; "economics" has been common since the late 19th century. It derives from Ancient Greek *oikonomia*, the "way (*nomos*) to run a household (*oikos*)." Some economic historians have called the Boeotian poet [[Hesiod]] the "first economist" for his writings on the distribution of resources.

Definitions have varied. [[Adam Smith]] (1776) described political economy as "an inquiry into the nature and causes of the wealth of nations," Jean-Baptiste Say (1803) as the science of the production, distribution, and consumption of wealth, and [[John Stuart Mill]] (1844) as the science tracing the laws of social phenomena arising from the combined operations of mankind for the production of wealth. Thomas Carlyle (1849) coined "the dismal science" as an epithet for classical economics. Alfred Marshall (1890) called economics "a study of man in the ordinary business of life." Lionel Robbins (1932) defined it as "the science which studies human behaviour as a relationship between ends and scarce means which have alternative uses," a formulation that has been called perhaps the most commonly accepted. Because it focuses on a form of behavior rather than a subject matter, it allowed economic analysis to extend to areas such as war; from the 1960s, rational-choice modeling, advanced by Gary Becker among others, broadened the field further. Critics have called the definition overly broad or noted that scarcity does not account for the macroeconomics of high unemployment. Several Nobel Prize winners, including James M. Buchanan and Ronald Coase, rejected Robbins's method-based definition in favor of subject-matter definitions like Say's, and Ha-Joon Chang has argued that no other science defines itself by a single method.

Schools of thought include the classical, neoclassical, Keynesian, and Austrian, which offer differing explanations of growth, business cycles, and the effects of policy. Economics informs policy on trade, money, and regulation, and economists continue to debate its predictive record, including its failure to anticipate the 2008 crisis, and the influence of ideology on the field.""".replace("[[Nobel Prize winners","[[Nobel Prize winners").replace("Several Nobel Prize winners","Several [[Nobel Prize|Nobel Prize]] winners").replace("inflation, economic growth","[[Inflation|inflation]], economic growth"))

a("Economy_8afc1f","Economy","economy","""Area of the production, distribution, and trade, as well as consumption, of goods and services. An economy is a set of interrelated human practices and transactions that does not stand alone: its main factors include culture, values, education, technological evolution, history, social organization, political structure, legal systems, and natural resources. Economic agents can be individuals, businesses, organizations, or governments. Transactions occur when two parties agree on the value or price of a good or service, commonly expressed in a [[Currency|currency]], although monetary transactions account for only a small part of the economic domain. Activity is spurred by production, which uses natural resources, labor, and capital, and it has changed over time with technology and innovation.

The word derives from Middle French *yconomie*, Medieval Latin *oeconomia*, and Ancient Greek *oikonomia*, from *oikos* ("house") and *nemein* ("to manage"). Its current sense, the economic system of a country or area, developed only in the 1650s.

Economies are as old as the making and exchange of goods. [[Sumer]] developed a large-scale economy based on commodity money, and the [[Babylonia|Babylonians]] and neighboring city-states developed rules and laws on debt, legal contracts, and private property. The shekel, first used in Mesopotamia c. 3000 BC, referred to a specific mass of barley and served as both a unit of weight and a currency. The ancient economy rested primarily on subsistence farming. In the Middle Ages most exchange occurred within social groups, but voyages by Marco Polo, Christopher Columbus, and Vasco da Gama led to a first global economy. The first [[Stock exchange|stock exchange]] was founded in Antwerp in 1513, and the European nation-states tried to control trade through customs duties and mercantilism.

[[Adam Smith]] is regarded as the first economist in the modern sense. He held that products are offered at a natural price generated by competition, or [[Supply and demand|supply and demand]], and by the division of labor, and that human self-interest is the basic motive for free trade. The [[Industrial Revolution]] of the 18th and 19th centuries brought major changes in agriculture, manufacturing, mining, and transport.

Modern economies are classified as market systems, in which decentralized decisions coordinated by prices allocate resources; command systems, dominated by central planning; and mixed systems. Activity is commonly measured by [[Gross domestic product|gross domestic product]] (GDP), the total market value of final goods and services produced. Critics note that GDP overlooks unpaid household labor, environmental degradation, and non-market well-being.""")

a("Ecosystem_47e317","Ecosystem","ecosystem","""Community of living organisms together with the nonliving components of their environment, linked through nutrient cycles and energy flows. An ecosystem consists of all the organisms and the abiotic pools, or physical environment, with which they interact. Ecosystem processes are the transfers of energy and materials from one pool to another and take place across a wide range of scales, from a small pond to a vast biome.

The term was first used in 1935 in a publication by the British ecologist Arthur Tansley. It was coined by Arthur Roy Clapham at Tansley's request, and Tansley devised the concept to draw attention to the transfers of materials between organisms and their environment. G. Evelyn Hutchinson proposed that mineral nutrient availability in a lake limited algal production and, in turn, the abundance of animals that feed on algae. Raymond Lindeman suggested that the flow of energy was the primary driver of the ecosystem, and Hutchinson's students Howard T. Odum and Eugene P. Odum developed a "systems approach" to studying it.

Ecosystems are controlled by external and internal factors. External factors, including [[Climate|climate]], control the ecosystem's structure but are not influenced by it; climate determines the [[Biome|biome]] in which an ecosystem is embedded. Internal factors both control and are controlled by ecosystem processes, and include [[Decomposition|decomposition]], the types of species present, root competition, shading, disturbance, and succession. Ecosystems are dynamic and always recovering from past disturbances. Their tendency to remain near equilibrium is termed resistance, and their capacity to absorb disturbance and reorganize while retaining the same function, structure, and identity is termed ecological resilience.

Plants allow energy to enter the system through [[Photosynthesis|photosynthesis]]. Animals move matter and energy through the system by feeding on plants and one another, and decomposers, such as bacteria and fungi, break down dead organic matter, returning carbon to the atmosphere and making nutrients available again to plants and microbes.

Ecosystems provide goods such as water, food, fuel, construction material, and medicinal plants, and services such as maintaining hydrological cycles, cleaning air and water, maintaining atmospheric oxygen, and crop [[Pollination|pollination]]. Many are degraded by human impacts including soil loss, air and water [[Pollution|pollution]], habitat fragmentation, water diversion, fire suppression, and [[Invasive species|invasive species]]. An ecosystem that has lost its defining features is considered "collapsed." Ecosystem restoration can contribute to achieving the [[Sustainable development|Sustainable Development]] Goals.""")

a("Education_de7a22","Education","education","""Transmission of knowledge and skills and the development of character traits. The term derives from the Latin *educare* ("to bring up") and *educere* ("to bring forth"), and it can refer to a process (schooling, teaching, and learning), to the mental states and qualities of educated people, or to the academic field that studies the methods, processes, and social institutions involved. Its exact definition is disputed, as are its aims.

Education is classified in several ways. Formal education happens in a complex institutional framework, such as public schools, with classes based on a student's age and progress. Non-formal education is also structured but occurs outside the formal schooling system, and informal education is unstructured learning through daily experiences. Formal and non-formal education are divided into levels: [[Early childhood education|early childhood]], [[Primary education|primary]], [[Secondary education|secondary]], and [[Tertiary education|tertiary]] education. Further classifications are based on teaching method (teacher-centered or student-centered), subject, medium, and funding.

Theorists have defined education in different ways. R. S. Peters outlined three essential features: that knowledge and understanding are imparted, that the process is beneficial, and that it is done in a morally appropriate manner. Critics note that such precise definitions exclude less common types of education, and alternatives rely on family resemblance among forms of education instead. Evaluative or thick conceptions hold that education by nature leads to some improvement, whereas thin conceptions are value-neutral. Many scholars stress [[Critical thinking|critical thinking]] as what distinguishes education from indoctrination, which instills beliefs regardless of whether they are rational. Others argue that the two cannot always be clearly separated, since young children must sometimes learn things, such as safety rules and hygiene practices, without understanding the reasons.

Education socializes children into society by teaching cultural values and norms and equips them with skills to become productive members of society. It stimulates [[Economic growth|economic growth]] and raises awareness of local and global problems; studies estimate that each additional year of schooling raises lifetime earnings by about 10%. Governments set education policy, determining when classes happen, what is taught, and who can or must attend, and international organizations such as [[UNESCO]] have promoted primary education for all children. Success depends on psychological factors (motivation, intelligence, and personality), social factors (socioeconomic status, ethnicity, and gender, which are often linked to discrimination), access to educational technology, teacher quality, and parent involvement. Some analyses find no systematic link between increased spending and student performance, attributing gaps to instructional quality, family background, and institutional incentives.

In prehistory, education was informal, taking place through oral communication and imitation. With the rise of ancient civilizations and the invention of writing, education shifted toward formal instruction, initially available mainly to elites and religious groups. The [[Printing press|printing press]] of the 15th century made books more widely available and increased literacy. From the 18th and 19th centuries public education became more important, leading to the worldwide effort to make primary education free and compulsory up to a certain age. Over 90% of primary-school-age children worldwide now attend primary school.""")

a("Egg_f04ba3","Egg","egg","""Organic vessel grown by an animal to carry a possibly fertilized [[Egg cell|egg cell]], or zygote, within which an [[Embryo|embryo]] is incubated until it can survive on its own and hatches. Reproductive structures similar to the egg in other kingdoms are called spores, or, in seed plants, seeds.

Most arthropods, vertebrates (excluding live-bearing mammals), and mollusks lay eggs, although some animals, such as scorpions, do not. Reptile, bird, and monotreme eggs are laid out of water and are surrounded by a protective shell, either flexible or inflexible. Some embryos have a temporary egg tooth that they use to break the shell when hatching.

The largest recorded egg came from a [[Whale shark|whale shark]] and measured 30 cm × 14 cm × 9 cm. The [[Ostrich|ostrich]] egg, at 1.5 kg and up to 17.8 cm × 14 cm, is the largest of any living bird, though the extinct elephant bird and some non-avian dinosaurs laid larger ones. The bee hummingbird lays the smallest known bird egg, 6.35–11.4 mm long and weighing half a gram.

Most [[Fish|fish]] are oviparous: the female lays undeveloped eggs that are fertilized externally, often in large numbers (large fish can produce over 100 million eggs in one spawning), and the eggs develop without parental care. Rays and most sharks are ovoviviparous, with the eggs fertilized and developing internally. Fish and amphibian eggs are jellylike, have no shell, and would dry out in air, so even air-breathing amphibians lay them in water or protective foam.

Amniotes, the air-breathing vertebrates comprising [[Reptile|reptiles]], birds, and mammals, have complex eggs or embryos with an amniotic membrane. Their eggs require internal fertilization, and the shell isolates the embryo from the mother. Reptile eggs are leathery in snakes and most lizards and calcareous in turtles; most are left on land by the parents, and in turtles, tuatara, and most lizards the temperature of the surroundings determines the embryo's sex. Dinosaurs laid eggs, some preserved as fossils. Among mammals, only the [[Monotreme|monotremes]], the platypus and two genera of echidna, lay eggs.

A typical [[Chicken|chicken]] egg has a hard calcium carbonate shell with about 17,000 microscopic pores for gas exchange, a protective cuticle, two inner membranes, albumen (egg white), and a yolk anchored by chalazae. Most commercial chicken eggs are unfertilized, and fertilized eggs need about 21 days of incubation to produce a chick.

Eggs are a popular food worldwide, with global production of approximately 91 million metric tons in 2023. They are also symbols of life, healing, and rebirth in folklore and mythology, and are frequently decorated. Egg collecting, once popular in some cultures, is now banned in many jurisdictions. Chicken eggs are used to produce vaccines for infectious diseases.""")

